{
  "id": "COMPOUND:16590 - PROTEIN:3694",
  "verbalization": "Gerontoisoflavone A appears to act as a target for multiple kinases, including Tyrosine-protein kinase Blk, Dual specificity protein kinase CLK2, and Serine/threonine-protein kinase 33, all of which are targeted by the same compound. These kinases are ultimately linked to Deoxyuridine 5'-triphosphate nucleotidohydrolase, putative, through a common intermediate, 4-(3-Pyridin-2-Yl-1h-Pyrazol-4-Yl)Quinoline. This suggests a potential mechanism where Gerontoisoflavone A modulates the activity of these kinases, which in turn influence the activity of the nucleotidohydrolase enzyme. The shared pathway indicates that the compound may exert its effects through a common downstream target, potentially affecting nucleotide metabolism or related processes. The evidence supports a role for Gerontoisoflavone A in regulating the activity of the nucleotidohydrolase enzyme, likely through the activation or inhibition of upstream kinases.",
